<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-renaissance-and-reformation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between roughly 1350 and 1650, Europe changed in two connected convulsions. The first, the
    <strong>Renaissance</strong> ("rebirth"), was not a light switch from "dark" to "bright" —
    medieval Europe was never a thousand years of nothing — but a period when <strong>wealth,
    recovered classical texts and new technology compounded in the cities of Italy</strong> and
    then spilled across the continent, changing art, learning, politics and how people saw
    themselves. The second, the <strong>Reformation</strong> (from 1517), used the new technology
    of printing to shatter the religious unity of western Christendom into Catholic and
    Protestant halves. Together they made much of the modern West — its science, its state
    system, its individualism — possible, at the price of a century and a half of religious war.
  </p>
  <p>
    The word "rebirth" was the period's own advertising: thinkers like Petrarch (1304–1374)
    believed they were reopening a window the "middle" ages had shut on classical Greece and
    Rome. Modern historians are more sceptical of the label — continuity from medieval
    universities and banks is everywhere — but the label survives because it points at something
    real: a self-conscious cultural movement that treated antiquity as a standard to imitate and
    exceed, and a religious revolution that treated the last 1,400 years of church tradition as
    something to strip away and rebuild from scripture. This chapter follows both, and the
    machine that connected them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The widget below gives the four drivers: banking wealth, the classical revival, the new
    mathematics of seeing, and the printing-fuelled split of the Church.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four drivers, from banking to Bible printing" widget="equationStep" config={renCfg} />

  <h2>Why Italy: wealth, cities and patronage</h2>
  <p>
    The Renaissance began in Italy for concrete economic reasons. Italian city-states —
    <strong>Florence, Venice, Milan, Genoa</strong> — sat at the crossroads of Mediterranean
    trade and grew rich on banking, wool and shipping. The <strong>Medici</strong> family of
    Florence built a Europe-wide banking fortune from the 1390s and then spent much of it on
    artists, architects and scholars: Cosimo de' Medici (died 1464) and his grandson Lorenzo the
    Magnificent funded Donatello, Botticelli and the young Michelangelo. In Rome, warrior popes
    like Julius II hired Raphael to fresco their apartments and Michelangelo to paint the Sistine
    ceiling (1508–1512). This system is called <strong>patronage</strong>, and it is the honest
    engine behind the "genius" story: art at this scale was expensive — pigments, marble, wages —
    and it was bought, commissioned and competed over, with city-states, guilds and families
    using buildings and paintings exactly as rivals use skyline towers today.
  </p>
  <p>
    Competition had a second, darker productivity. Italy was politically fragmented, and its
    small states lived by diplomacy, mercenary armies and reputation. Out of that world came
    Niccolò Machiavelli's <em>The Prince</em> (written 1513): the first handbook of politics as
    it is actually practised — advising rulers to be prepared to break promises when survival
    requires it — rather than as moralists said it should be. It is a perfect emblem of the
    era's habit of turning back to ancient examples (Rome, Sparta) to think about present power
    in secular terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Humanism: the intellectual programme</h2>
  <p>
    The movement's mind was <strong>humanism</strong> — from <em>studia humanitatis</em>, the
    study of grammar, rhetoric, history, poetry and moral philosophy, the subjects that ancient
    education had centred on. Humanists were not anti-religion (almost all were devout); their
    programme was to recover, edit and <em>read properly</em> the texts of antiquity and of early
    Christianity. Petrarch hunted manuscripts; Lorenzo Valla proved in 1440 that the
    <em>Donation of Constantine</em> — the document by which the pope supposedly received temporal
    power — was a forgery, by noticing its Latin could not be fourth-century. This is critical
    philology, the ancestor of modern source criticism, and it quietly taught Europe that
    authorities could be checked against evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two consequences matter for everything that follows. First, the recovery of ancient science:
    when the Ottoman siege of Constantinople ended in 1453, Greek scholars and manuscripts moved
    west, including mathematical and astronomical texts the Latin West had lost or never had.
    Copernicus's heliocentric proposal of 1543 and Galileo's mechanics grow out of this recovered
    material. Second, a shift of emphasis — from humanity's misery and distance from God toward
    human dignity and potential. The Florentine Pico della Mirandola's <em>Oration on the
    Dignity of Man</em> (1486) made man the free shaper of his own nature. It is a long way from
    modern individualism — but you can see the road.
  </p>

  <h2>Art and the mathematics of seeing</h2>
  <p>
    Renaissance painting looks different from medieval painting for a technical reason, not just
    a taste. Around 1420 the Florentine architect <strong>Brunelleschi</strong> demonstrated
    <strong>linear perspective</strong>: a geometric method for projecting three-dimensional
    space onto a flat panel so that parallel lines converge on vanishing points, giving
    mathematically correct depth. Within a generation painters like Masaccio and Alberti (whose
    1435 treatise codified it) were building pictures like engineers — some early works were laid
    out with grids and nails, and artists studied anatomy through dissection to get bodies right.
    Brunelleschi's crowning engineering feat was the dome of Florence cathedral (completed 1436),
    raised without centring scaffolds by a double-shell design nobody had dared before.
  </p>
  <p>
    The roll call shows the fusion of art and craft knowledge: <strong>Leonardo da Vinci</strong>
    (painter, anatomist, hydraulic engineer, military consultant), <strong>Michelangelo</strong>
    (sculptor of the <em>David</em> and painter of the Sistine vault), <strong>Raphael</strong>,
    <strong>Titian</strong> in Venice, and north of the Alps <strong>Dürer</strong>, who carried
    perspective and humanist print culture into Germany. Art history's point stands: painting
    became partly a scientific discipline, and the artist rose from craftsman to celebrity genius
    — a new social role with a long future.
  </p>

  <h2>The printing press: the real protagonist</h2>
  <p>
    Around 1450, <strong>Johannes Gutenberg</strong> of Mainz combined movable metal type, an
    oil-based ink and a converted wine press into a practical printing system. The numbers tell
    the story of diffusion: by 1500, roughly 1,000 European print shops had produced an estimated
    20 million books — more than all the scribes of the previous thousand years. Costs collapsed
    (a book fell from roughly the price of a small farm to that of a few days' wages over the
    following century), and, crucially, identical copies meant that a text and an idea could now
    circulate without being silently altered in transmission.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last property is what made the <strong>Reformation</strong> possible. On 31 October
    1517, Martin Luther, an Augustinian friar and university professor in Wittenberg, circulated
    his <strong>Ninety-five Theses</strong> attacking the sale of <strong>indulgences</strong> —
    payments that, as marketed by the friar Johann Tetzel to fund the rebuilding of St Peter's in
    Rome, promised release from punishment for sin. The theses were a routine academic debate
    invitation by the standards of the day; the printing press made them a European event.
    Luther's subsequent pamphlets sold in the hundreds of thousands — his 1520 treatises alone
    roughly 300,000 copies in three years — while Rome's replies had to be smuggled in and could
    not be out-printed. Vernacular Bibles followed (Luther's German New Testament, 1522, sold
    thousands of copies within weeks), which meant laypeople could read scripture in their own
    language, a change with consequences far beyond theology.
  </p>

  <h2>The Reformation: doctrine, split and wars</h2>
  <p>
    Luther's core positions were radical: <strong>salvation by faith alone</strong> (not by works,
    payments or pilgrimages), the <strong>Bible alone</strong> as religious authority (not pope
    or councils), and the priesthood of all believers. Excommunicated in 1521 after refusing to
    recant at the Diet of Worms, he was protected by Frederick the Wise of Saxony — German
    princes saw in "Reformation" both conscience and a chance to shake off church taxes and
    Rome's power. The split spread in variants: Zwingli in Zurich, the stern city theocracy of
    Calvin in Geneva (from the 1540s), the radical Anabaptists, and, in England, a church broken
    from Rome by Henry VIII for reasons of dynasty as much as doctrine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Catholic response was the <strong>Counter-Reformation</strong>: the Council of Trent
    (1545–1563) reformed abuses and reaffirmed doctrine, the new Jesuit order re-conquered minds
    through schools and missions, and the Roman Inquisition enforced the line — Galileo's
    condemnation (1633) belongs to this machinery. The result was a century and a half of
    religious conflict: the German Peasants' War (1524–25), France's Wars of Religion
    (1562–1598), the Dutch revolt against Spain, and finally the <strong>Thirty Years' War</strong>
    (1618–1648), which devastated the Holy Roman Empire — some regions of Germany lost a third or
    more of their population. Its settlement, the <strong>Peace of Westphalia</strong> (1648),
    effectively recognised permanent religious pluralism and sovereign states as the new order of
    Europe: the modern state system was built on the wreckage of Christendom's unity.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Reformation was not simply "the people versus a corrupt
  church", nor Luther a lone democrat before his time. Princes, cities and printers had
  independent motives — property, power, profit — and peasant radicals who took Luther's Bible
  into social revolt were crushed, by Lutheran princes among others. Reformation success tracked
  political protection as much as theological persuasion.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Historiographically, the period is a minefield the careful reader learns to walk. The term
    "Renaissance" was popularised by Jacob Burckhardt's <em>Civilisation of the Renaissance in
    Italy</em> (1860), which coined "individualism" for its spirit; twentieth-century historians
    then found most of Burckhardt's "Renaissance" traits in thirteenth-century proto-urban
    Europe, and medievalists like Johan Huizinga argued the fifteenth century north of the Alps
    was still intensely "medieval" in feeling. The current consensus treats the Renaissance as a
    real but regional and uneven movement — brilliant in Tuscan art and humanist scholarship,
    thin elsewhere — and the Reformation as the more genuinely continent-wide transformation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitative details sharpen the picture of printing's revolution. Venice and not Germany
    became the industry's capital (the Aldine press printed Greek classics in pocket editions);
    print runs of a few hundred copies made ideas reproducible at a scale manuscript culture
    could never match; and censorship created the <em>Index of Prohibited Books</em> (1559) —
    an acknowledgement that controlling the press now mattered as much as controlling armies.
    Eisenstein's classic argument is that print created "fixed" standard editions, front matter
    and citation habits — the preconditions of modern scholarship and, eventually, of the
    scientific revolution's cumulative progress. Open questions remain about how much Renaissance
    learning (especially esoteric and astrological strands) fed into early modern science, and
    about the movement's blind spots: women gained access to humanist education only
    exceptionally (Isotta Nogarola, Christine de Pizan earlier, and later a small circle of
    learned women), and the era's art was partly financed by Atlantic colonisation that the
    humanists rarely examined. The rebirth, in other words, had an inside and an outside, and
    full command of the unit means holding both.
  </p>
  <p>
    A final analytical habit completes the chapter: separating <em>cause</em> from
    <em>condition</em> across the two movements. The printing press was a condition — it made
    rapid, identical transmission possible — but the Reformation's cause still required the
    theological grievance (indulgences, papal authority) and the political protection (Frederick
    of Saxony's territory). Likewise, Italian wealth was a condition for Renaissance art, but
    the cause ran through taste and competition — patrons chose antiquity because it carried
    civic prestige in city-states that imagined themselves as new Romes. This is the same
    trigger–precondition–contingency discipline the historical-method lesson introduces, and it
    dissolves the two most common exam errors: crediting a technology with a movement (print
    "caused" the Reformation) and crediting a movement with a technology's consequences (the
    Renaissance "caused" modern science, when the chain runs through many hands and two
    centuries). Wealth bought the canvas, the press multiplied the page — but ideas, interests
    and decisions aimed them both, and the historian's job is to keep all three columns of the
    ledger open at once.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Burke, The Italian Renaissance: Culture and Society in Italy; Eisenstein, The Printing Press as an Agent of Change (1979); MacCulloch, The Reformation (2003); Burckhardt, The Civilisation of the Renaissance in Italy (1860); Grafton, Defenders of the Text.</p>
